@souredoutlook another customer reported the issue via Slack. 

In addition to what Lingvistion mentioned, users expect the transition from "Archived" to "Escalated" to trigger an alert. Also, if this is "working as expected" there is no alert option to trigger an alert when an issue goes from "Archived" to "Ongoing". It makes the option to "archive until it happens X times" not very useful.

Can we get a re-evaluation on the "expected" behaviour here?

@rcohen12000 referencing the issues linked in the support ticket, with some examples here:

For the event which does not work, the base image address is `0x1d2ee4000` with a size of `53379072` bytes, this marks the end of the image at `0x1d61cc000`.  Looking at a frame which is _not_ symbolicated, the instruction address is at `0x1d81d39b6` which is past the module. For that frame no image is available to lookup any unwind/debug information.

There also is an error on how you build the image (you can see that by checking the JSON of the issue):

```json
"errors": [
        {
              "type": "missing_attribute",
              "name": "debug_meta.images.0.code_file"
        }
  ],
```

@cmanallen how much clock drift should we accept/allow?

Requesting specific IDs is tough because in some other cases we might have an id but there is no error for it. What we're doing now is assuming we'll get N errors (for N id's in the error_ids list) and then checking to see if there are any extra errors within the timebounds. Time bounds come from the replayRecord, which should be related to the min/max timestamp from the list of replaySegments.

What I'm thinking is that we can expand the timebounds to add a buffer, then if the clock drift is within that buffer we'll still find the errors. In the UI we'll also endup adjusting the start/end times of the replay so those bounds include the extra errors.

